        {
          "post_id": "490384",
          "text": "<p>An algorithm performs a linear search for an element $k$ in an integer array of size $N$. The algorithm iterates through the array sequentially, starting from the first element, and stops as soon as $k$ is found or the end of the array is reached. Let $T(N)$ denote the time complexity function of this algorithm.</p><p>Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply)</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$\\mathrm{T}(\\mathrm{N})$ is $O(N)$ but not $\\Omega(1)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\mathrm{T}(\\mathrm{N})$ is $O(1)$ but not $\\Omega(N)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\mathrm{T}(\\mathrm{N})$ is both $O(N)$ and $\\Omega(1)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\mathrm{T(N)}$ is both $O(N)$ and $\\Omega(N)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Algorithms",
          "day": "Day 101"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "490382",
          "text": "<p>Let $S$ be a sorted array of $n$ distinct positive integers. Let $T(n)$ denote the time complexity of the most efficient algorithm to determine if there exist three indices $i&lt;j&lt;k$ such that the condition $S[i]+S[j]&gt;S[k]$ is met.<br>&nbsp;<br>Which of the following is true?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$T(n)=O\\left(n^3\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$T(n)=O\\left(n^2\\right)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$T(n)=O(n \\log n)$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$T(n)=O(n)$</li></ol>",
